P={
"Hagia Sophia":dict(steps=["Justinian ordered rebuilding weeks after rioters burned the previous church in the Nika riots of 532.","Four huge piers and great arches formed a square base about 31 m across.","Pendentives — curved triangular sections — made the transition from the square base to the circular dome, a breakthrough in architecture.","The shallow dome of light bricks rose on 40 ribs, with 40 windows at its base that make it seem to float on a ring of light.","Two semi-domes on east and west extended the nave into a vast open space.","Walls were covered in coloured marble and gold mosaics; legend says Justinian declared, 'Solomon, I have surpassed you!'"]),
"Petra":dict(summary="Petra, the Nabataean capital, was a rich caravan city controlling incense and spice routes between Arabia and the Mediterranean. Its most famous monuments, like Al-Khazneh ('the Treasury') and the Monastery, are carved directly into rose-red sandstone cliffs.",tricks="The Nabataeans were masters of desert water: dams diverted flash floods away from the narrow Siq gorge, and clay pipes and rock-cut channels fed cisterns, pools and even a garden with a swimming pool, supplying about 20,000–30,000 people. Earthquakes in 363 CE damaged the water system, and the city declined; the outside world rediscovered it in 1812 when Swiss explorer Johann Ludwig Burckhardt visited disguised as a pilgrim."),
"Machu Picchu":dict(tricks="Inca walls use 'ashlar' masonry, blocks fitted so tightly without mortar that they move during earthquakes and settle back. Engineers built extensive drainage: layers of topsoil, sand, gravel and rock in terraces, and 129 drainage channels, which is why the site has survived heavy rains for 500 years. It was abandoned around the Spanish conquest, and Hiram Bingham publicised it in 1911, although local farmers knew it well."),
"Angkor water system":dict(summary="Angkor, capital of the Khmer Empire, was a 'hydraulic city' covering about 1,000 km² — perhaps the largest pre-industrial urban complex. A network of reservoirs (barays), canals and moats captured monsoon rain to support rice farming and the temples.",tricks="Angkor Wat's 5–10 million sandstone blocks were floated on canals from quarries at Phnom Kulen about 40 km away; marks on stones suggest a precise system. The moat around Angkor Wat keeps the groundwater stable, stopping the sandy foundation from shifting. Later, alternating mega-droughts and floods in the 14th–15th centuries damaged the system, contributing to Angkor's decline."),
"Chand Baori stepwell":dict(summary="Built by King Chanda of the Nikumbha dynasty in the 9th century, Chand Baori in Abhaneri, Rajasthan, is one of the deepest and largest stepwells in India: about 3,500 narrow steps over 13 storeys descend 30 m to the water. Its geometric pattern of steps is mesmerising.",steps=["A square shaft about 35 m wide was dug into the ground to reach groundwater.","Double flights of steps were built in a zigzag on three sides, letting many people reach the water at once.","On the fourth side, pillared pavilions and royal chambers were built for rest.","A temple to Harshat Mata was built next to it."],tricks="Because air at the bottom is 5–6 °C cooler than at the surface, the stepwell served as a gathering place in hot summers. Stepwells were important to women, who fetched water, and many were built by queens."),
"Golconda Fort acoustics":dict(summary="Golconda began as a Kakatiya mud fort and became the capital of the Qutb Shahi sultans (1518–1687), famous for its diamond trade — the Koh-i-Noor and Hope diamond came from nearby mines. Its acoustic design lets a hand clap at the Fateh Darwaza entrance be heard at the Bala Hisar pavilion about 1 km away.",steps=["Massive granite walls (about 10 km in total) with 87 bastions were built around a 120 m granite hill.","Gates were studded with iron spikes against war elephants.","Persian wheels and terracotta pipes lifted water uphill to the palaces and gardens.","Domes and corridors were shaped to carry sound, serving as an alarm system."],tricks="Aurangzeb took the fort in 1687 after an eight-month siege, only after a traitor opened a gate."),
"Taj Mahal":dict(summary="Shah Jahan built the Taj Mahal as a tomb for his wife Mumtaz Mahal, who died in 1631. Considered the jewel of Mughal architecture, it combines Persian, Central Asian and Indian elements and changes colour with the light.",steps=["The foundation on the Yamuna's bank used wells filled with rubble and ebony and mahogany wood, which stay strong when kept moist by the river.","A brick core was faced with white Makrana marble brought from Rajasthan by about 1,000 elephants.","A double dome makes the outer dome tall while keeping the inner chamber proportional.","Pietra dura: semi-precious stones like jade, lapis lazuli and carnelian were inlaid in floral designs.","Four minarets were built leaning slightly outward."],tricks="The minarets tilt outward so that in an earthquake they would fall away from the tomb. Calligraphy grows larger towards the top so it appears the same size from the ground. Air pollution has yellowed the marble, so it is cleaned with a mud pack (multani mitti)."),
"Konark Sun Temple":dict(summary="Built around 1250 by Narasimhadeva I of the Eastern Ganga dynasty, the Konark Sun Temple is shaped like the Sun god Surya's giant chariot, with 24 carved stone wheels pulled by seven horses. Its main tower, which collapsed long ago, may have been about 70 m tall.",steps=["The temple was built of khondalite stone on a platform near the sea.","Iron beams were used inside the structure; one, about 10.6 m long, is among the largest forged iron beams of the era.","The 24 wheels were carved with spokes acting as sundials.","Walls were covered in carvings of daily life, music, war and love.","Legend says a powerful magnet at the top once caused ships' compasses to fail."],tricks="The wheels' shadows can tell the time accurately. The main sanctum was filled with sand by the British in 1903 to stop it collapsing."),
"Pont du Gard":dict(tricks="The whole 50 km aqueduct from Uzès to Nîmes falls just 12.6 m — about 25 cm per kilometre — showing extraordinary surveying precision. It carried about 40,000 m³ of water a day. Builders left protruding stones and holes for scaffolding and inscribed numbers on blocks to guide assembly."),
"Iron Bridge":dict(summary="Built over the River Severn at Coalbrookdale, where Abraham Darby I had pioneered smelting iron with coke in 1709, the Iron Bridge (1779) was the first major bridge made of cast iron. It became a symbol of the Industrial Revolution."),
"Forth Bridge":dict(summary="After the Tay Bridge collapsed in a storm in 1879, killing about 75 people on a train, Scottish engineers built the Forth Bridge to be visibly massive and safe. Opened in 1890, it was the world's longest cantilever bridge and carries trains to this day.",steps=["Three huge double cantilevers ('balanced arms') were built out from three main piers.","Tubular steel members, some 3.7 m in diameter, gave strength.","Suspended girder spans connected the cantilevers.","The bridge was painted constantly, giving rise to the phrase 'like painting the Forth Bridge' for an endless task."]),
"Golden Gate Bridge":dict(summary="Built during the Great Depression across the Golden Gate strait with its strong currents and fog, the Golden Gate Bridge was the world's longest suspension span (1,280 m) when it opened in 1937. Its 'International Orange' colour makes it visible in fog.",steps=["The south tower foundation was built in open sea, inside a concrete fender.","Steel towers rose 227 m.","Each main cable was spun from 27,572 wires.","The deck was hung from suspender cables."]),
"Howrah Bridge":dict(summary="Built during World War II to replace a floating pontoon bridge over the Hooghly, the Howrah Bridge (Rabindra Setu) is a cantilever bridge riveted without nuts and bolts, using steel mostly from Tata Steel. It opened in 1943 without a ceremony, fearing Japanese bombing.",steps=["Two 85 m high towers were built on both banks.","Anchor arms and cantilever arms were extended out over the river.","A suspended span joined the two cantilevers in the middle.","The whole structure was riveted together."]),
"Pamban Bridge":dict(summary="Opened in 1914, the Pamban Bridge links Rameswaram island to mainland India across the Palk Strait. It was India's first sea bridge, with a central Scherzer rolling lift span opening for ships. A new vertical-lift bridge opened alongside it in 2025.",steps=["Piers were built in a corrosive marine environment.","A double-leaf bascule span opened manually to let ships pass.","The 2025 bridge uses a 72 m vertical lift span raised electromechanically."]),
"Chenab Rail Bridge":dict(summary="On the Udhampur–Srinagar–Baramulla railway, the Chenab Bridge spans a deep gorge with a 467 m steel arch, 359 m above the river — higher than the Eiffel Tower. It opened in 2025, linking Kashmir by rail.",steps=["Two cable cranes, with the world's largest such pylons, carried materials across the gorge.","The arch was built out from both sides in segments, held back by temporary cables.","The arch closed in April 2021.","The deck was launched over the arch."]),
"Living root bridges":dict(summary="In the rain-soaked hills of Meghalaya, Khasi and Jaintia communities grow bridges from the roots of the rubber fig tree. Unlike timber, which rots, these living bridges grow stronger over time and can last centuries."),
"Tacoma Narrows Bridge collapse":dict(summary="Opened in July 1940, the slender first Tacoma Narrows Bridge swayed so much in wind that it was nicknamed 'Galloping Gertie'. On 7 November 1940, in a 64 km/h wind, it twisted violently and collapsed into Puget Sound — captured on film.",steps=["Designers used a shallow plate-girder deck instead of a deep truss, to save money and look elegant.","Wind caused vertical oscillations from the start; motorists enjoyed the ride.","On 7 November wind triggered torsional flutter — self-reinforcing twisting.","A cable band slipped and the deck tore apart."]),
"Okinawa caves and Japanese underground HQ":dict(summary="In the Battle of Okinawa (April–June 1945), the bloodiest of the Pacific War, Japanese forces turned Okinawa's natural limestone caves (gama) and dug tunnels into command posts, hospitals and shelters. Civilians also hid in caves, and their fate was tragic."),
"Maginot Line":dict(steps=["Underground forts (ouvrages) were built up to 30 m deep, with barracks, kitchens, power stations, hospitals and electric railways.","Retractable steel gun turrets rose to fire and dropped for protection.","Anti-tank obstacles and casemates covered the gaps.","It ended at the Belgian border, partly because Belgium was an ally and the Ardennes forest was thought impassable to tanks."]),
"Cheyenne Mountain Complex":dict(summary="Built during the Cold War inside a granite mountain near Colorado Springs, the Cheyenne Mountain Complex housed NORAD, which tracks missiles and aircraft over North America. It was designed to survive a nearby nuclear blast."),
"The Great Escape tunnel 'Harry'":dict(steps=["Prisoners dug three tunnels — Tom, Dick and Harry — so that if one was discovered, the others could continue.","Harry's entrance was hidden under a stove in Hut 104.","Walls were shored up with about 4,000 bed boards.","Air was pumped through pipes made of powdered-milk tins.","Sand was hidden in bags inside trousers and scattered in gardens ('penguins').","Forgers produced false passports and tailors made civilian clothes."]),
"Wootz steel":dict(summary="Wootz was a high-carbon crucible steel produced in South India and Sri Lanka from around 300 BCE. Traded to Persia and Syria, it was forged into famous 'Damascus' swords with wavy patterns, sharp and strong."),
"Qanat underground canals":dict(summary="Qanats are gently sloping underground tunnels that carry groundwater from mountain aquifers to fields and towns using gravity alone. Developed in Iran about 3,000 years ago, they spread to North Africa, Spain, China and India."),
"Yakhchāl ice houses and windcatchers":dict(summary="In the Persian desert, people made and stored ice through scorching summers using yakhchāls — domed ice houses with thick walls of insulating mortar — and cooled homes with windcatchers (badgirs) that funnel breezes."),
"Archimedes screw":dict(summary="A helix inside a tube lifts water when turned, used for irrigation and draining mines. Traditionally attributed to Archimedes, it may have been used earlier to water the gardens of Nineveh."),
"Dujiangyan irrigation system":dict(summary="Built in 256 BCE by Li Bing, governor of the Shu region for the state of Qin, Dujiangyan controls floods and irrigates the Chengdu plain without a dam — and still works after 2,270 years."),
"Indian muslin weaving":dict(summary="Bengali weavers spun phuti karpas cotton into yarn so fine that Dhaka muslin was called 'woven air'; a long sari could pass through a ring. It was prized by Romans, Mughals and European elites."),
}
